  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "This paper establishes a conceptual framework reinterpreting geoprivacy as a political and socio-technical issue rather than a purely technical problem of masking spatial coordinates. It analyzes how modern AI, platform dependencies, and corporate control convert pervasive location traces into sensitive inferences about identity, behavior, and social vulnerability. The authors propose systemic interventions including spatial inference regulation, privacy-protective defaults, public-good data governance, and community-controlled digital infrastructures.",
      "why": "It shifts the focus of location privacy from technical point-cloaking toward governing the inferred knowledge generated by spatial machine learning models. This challenges the standard GIScience approach of relying on consent forms or coordinate perturbation to protect user mobility data."
    }
